==Conduct==
Early voting for the first round was conducted from 8 to 10 May, during which more than 91,000 people, or 3.8% of eligible voters participated.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Over 91,000 Lithuanian voters cast ballots in 2 days of early voting |url=https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2268606/over-91-000-lithuanian-voters-cast-ballots-in-2-days-of-early-voting |access-date=2024-05-14 |website=LRT |date=9 May 2024 |language=en |archive-date=14 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240514061012/https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2268606/over-91-000-lithuanian-voters-cast-ballots-in-2-days-of-early-voting |url-status=live }}</ref> Voting for those who cannot leave their homes was held from 10 to 11 May.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Home voting begins in Lithuania's presidential election and citizenship referendum |url=https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2269628/home-voting-begins-in-lithuania-s-presidential-election-and-citizenship-referendum |access-date=2024-05-14 |website=LRT |date=10 May 2024 |language=en |archive-date=14 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240514061001/https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2269628/home-voting-begins-in-lithuania-s-presidential-election-and-citizenship-referendum |url-status=live }}</ref> On the first round of regular voting on 12 May, polling opened at 07:00 and lasted until 20:00 local time. The [[Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe]] did not send election monitors to Lithuania for the first time after it turned down an invitation by the government due to its refusal to admit monitors from Russia and Belarus on national security grounds, which the OSCE said was a violation of its rules.<ref name="ap" /> This led [[Ignas Vėgėlė]] to criticize what he called a "lack of transparency" due to their absence. Two thousand other monitors were allowed to observe the election.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lithuania's election chief rejects criticism over absence of OSCE observers |url=https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2271855/lithuania-s-election-chief-rejects-criticism-over-absence-of-osce-observers |access-date=2024-05-14 |website=LRT |date=13 May 2024 |language=en |archive-date=14 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240514060556/https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2271855/lithuania-s-election-chief-rejects-criticism-over-absence-of-osce-observers |url-status=live }}</ref> No election-related incidents were recorded by Lithuanian authorities.<ref name="turnout" />

For the second round, early voting began on 21 May and closed on 23 May. Voting in designated locations outside normal polling areas was held from 22 May to 25 May, while voting for those unable to vote in person was held from 24 May to 25 May. Turnout in early voting reached 11.03% of the electorate. On 26 May, polling was held from 07:00 to 20:00.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Early voting starts in Lithuania's presidential run-off |url=https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2278158/early-voting-starts-in-lithuania-s-presidential-run-off |access-date=2024-05-21 |website=LRT |date=21 May 2024 |language=en |archive-date=21 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521153843/https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2278158/early-voting-starts-in-lithuania-s-presidential-run-off |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Home voting starts in Lithuania's presidential run-off |url=https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2280993/home-voting-starts-in-lithuania-s-presidential-run-off |access-date=2024-05-25 |website=LRT |date=24 May 2024 |language=en |archive-date=24 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240524190439/https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2280993/home-voting-starts-in-lithuania-s-presidential-run-off |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Presidential election run-off underway in Lithuania |url=https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2278660/presidential-election-run-off-underway-in-lithuania |access-date=2024-05-26 |website=LRT |date=26 May 2024 |language=en |archive-date=26 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240526130439/https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2278660/presidential-election-run-off-underway-in-lithuania |url-status=live }}</ref> While voting was underway on 26 May, Nauseda was warned by the electoral commission after he issued a message on [[Facebook]] urging voters to await the results in the gardens of the [[Presidential Palace, Vilnius|presidential palace]], which the agency deemed to be a possible violation of the rule of silence during election time. His office denied that the post amounted to electoral campaigning and that he would not remove the post.<ref name="facebook">{{Cite web |title=Lithuanians vote in presidential runoff amid security fears over Russia |url=https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20240526-lithuanians-vote-in-presidential-runoff-amid-security-fears-over-russia |access-date=2024-05-26 |website=France 24 |date=26 May 2024 |language=en |archive-date=26 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240526153535/https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20240526-lithuanians-vote-in-presidential-runoff-amid-security-fears-over-russia |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Results==
[[File:Lithuanian presidential election (2024).jpg|thumb|250px|Lithuanian presidential election results (first round)<br>Legend:<br>
{{legend0|#FFA500}} – '''Gitanas Nausėda'''<br>
{{legend0|#00BFFF}} – '''Ingrida Šimonytė'''<br>
{{legend0|#BDB76B}} – '''Eduardas Vaitkus'''<br>
{{legend0|#D2B48C}} – '''Remigijus Žemaitaitis''']]
<section begin="Results"/>
No candidate received an absolute majority (over 50% of the votes cast) in the first round of voting on 12 May. A second round was held on 26 May. Of the eight candidates, incumbent President Gitanas Nausėda won 44.46% and Prime Minister Ingrida Šimonytė won 20.02% of votes in the first round, finishing first and second respectively, and they were the only two candidates to appear on the runoff ballot for the second round.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-05-13 |title=Lithuanian president is the front-runner as country heads to runoff vote in two weeks |url=https://apnews.com/article/lithuania-president-election-russia-ukraine-nato-95037574444eb542e309794ff33e0775 |access-date=2024-05-13 |website=AP News |language=en |archive-date=13 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240513171633/https://apnews.com/article/lithuania-president-election-russia-ukraine-nato-95037574444eb542e309794ff33e0775 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Lithuania: Nauseda and Simonyte head to presidential run-off |url=https://www.dw.com/en/lithuania-nauseda-and-simonyte-head-to-presidential-run-off/a-69055624 |access-date=2024-05-13 |website=Deutsche Welle |language=en |archive-date=13 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240513171633/https://www.dw.com/en/lithuania-nauseda-and-simonyte-head-to-presidential-run-off/a-69055624 |url-status=live }}</ref> Nausėda won the first round in all [[Municipalities of Lithuania|municipalities]] except in [[Vilnius]], which voted for Šimonytė, and [[Šalčininkai District Municipality|Šalčininkai]] and [[Visaginas Municipality|Visaginas]], which were won by [[Eduardas Vaitkus]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nausėda and Šimonytė make it to runoffs in rerun of 2019 presidential election |url=https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2270543/nauseda-and-simonyte-make-it-to-runoffs-in-rerun-of-2019-presidential-election |access-date=2024-05-14 |website=LRT |date=13 May 2024 |language=en |archive-date=14 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240514060023/https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2270543/nauseda-and-simonyte-make-it-to-runoffs-in-rerun-of-2019-presidential-election |url-status=live }}</ref> Turnout in the first round was at 59.37%, the third highest for an election in Lithuania since the [[1993 Lithuanian presidential election|1993]] and [[1997–98 Lithuanian presidential election|1997 presidential elections]].<ref name="turnout">{{Cite web |title=Lithuania sees nearly 60% turnout in presidential election, highest since 1997 |url=https://tvpworld.com/77494452/lithuania-sees-nearly-60-turnout-in-presidential-election-highest-since-1997 |access-date=2024-05-14 |website=TVP |language=en |archive-date=14 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240514052839/https://tvpworld.com/77494452/lithuania-sees-nearly-60-turnout-in-presidential-election-highest-since-1997 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

After the first round, Šimonytė was endorsed by Žalimas, and Nausėda was endorsed by Žemaitaitis and Vėgėlė.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Lyberytė |first1=Augustė |title=Vėgėlė antrame rinkimų ture reiškia paramą Nausėdai |url=https://www.lrt.lt/naujienos/lietuvoje/2/2280982/vegele-antrame-rinkimu-ture-reiskia-parama-nausedai |website=LRT |language=lt |date=24 May 2024 |access-date=24 May 2024 |archive-date=24 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240524072247/https://www.lrt.lt/naujienos/lietuvoje/2/2280982/vegele-antrame-rinkimu-ture-reiskia-parama-nausedai |url-status=live }}</ref> Vaitkus stated that he will [[Spoilt vote|spoil his ballot]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Jakučionis |first1=Saulius |last2=Venckūnas |first2=Vilmantas |title=Prasideda antrasis rinkimų turas – daugelis į jį nepatekusių kandidatų nenutarė, ką rems |url=https://www.lrt.lt/naujienos/lietuvoje/2/2277517/prasideda-antrasis-rinkimu-turas-daugelis-i-ji-nepatekusiu-kandidatu-nenutare-ka-rems |website=LRT |language=lt |date=21 May 2024 |access-date=21 May 2024 |archive-date=21 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521060314/https://www.lrt.lt/naujienos/lietuvoje/2/2277517/prasideda-antrasis-rinkimu-turas-daugelis-i-ji-nepatekusiu-kandidatu-nenutare-ka-rems |url-status=live }}</ref> The second round results showed Nausėda winning about three-quarters of the vote, allowing him to declare victory.<ref name="ajvictory" /> This was the highest majority for a presidential runoff in Lithuania.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-05-28 |title=Easy win and easier second term – what do Lithuanian presidential election results mean |url=https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2282730/easy-win-and-easier-second-term-what-do-lithuanian-presidential-election-results-mean |access-date=2024-05-28 |website=LRT |language=en |archive-date=27 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240527093842/https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2282730/easy-win-and-easier-second-term-what-do-lithuanian-presidential-election-results-mean |url-status=live }}</ref>

